                            Preamble

  The GNU General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other kinds of works.

  The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed
to take away your freedom to share and change the works.  By contrast,
the GNU General Public License is intended to guarantee your freedom to
share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it remains free
software for all its users.  We, the Free Software Foundation, use the
GNU General Public License for most of our software; it applies also to
any other work released this way by its authors.  You can apply it to
your programs, too.

  When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not
price.  Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you
have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge any
price or no price) for delivering to users, that you receive source
code or can get it if you want it, that you can change the software or
keep it for yourself, that you can run it for any purpose, and that you
cannot be prevented from doing so.

  Developers that use the GNU GPL protect your rights with two
steps: (1) assert copyright on the software, and (2) offer you this
License giving you legal permission to copy, distribute and/or modify
it.

  For the developers' and authors' protection, the GPL clearly
explains that there is no warranty for this free software.  For both
users' and authors' sake, the GPL requires that modified versions be
marked as changed, so that their problems will not be attributed
otherwise to authors of previous versions.

  Some devices are designed to deny users the freedom to install or
run modified versions of the software built into them, although the
manufacturer cannot do so.  This is incompatible with the aim of
protecting users' freedom to change the software.  The precise license
requirements are found in the "System Libraries" section.

  The Free Software Foundation has developed the GNU General Public
License to protect users' freedom to share and change software, and we
insist that any modified version of software that is distributed to
others must be offered under the same license.

  Finally, every program is at risk from software patents.  States
should not be allowed to grant patents that would restrict use, sale,
or importation of software, and we insist that all contributors grant
such patent licenses under the terms of this License.

